{:edb/db-before-t 831 :edb/db-after-t 832 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534144 50 #inst "2026-10-06T04:41:30.343Z" 13194139534144 true] [17592186059736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278576 13194139534144 true] [17592186059736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278583 13194139534144 true] [17592186059741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278569 13194139534144 true] [17592186059741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278586 13194139534144 true] [17592186059741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278593 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278570 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1089 "q-117412" 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1100 "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1113 #uuid "2dbc0e3f-52e0-5c91-bb08-00ee163a5979" 13194139534144 true] [17592186278569 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534144 true] [17592186278570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278571 13194139534144 true] [17592186278570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278572 13194139534144 true] [17592186278570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278573 13194139534144 true] [17592186278570 1000 #edb